    "title": "African Nations Renew Call for Permanent UN Security Council Seats",
    "original_title": "Afrika oo dalbanaysa kuraas joogto ah Golaha Ammaanka ah, balse dib-u-habayntu weli caqabad ku tahay",
    "slug": "african-nations-renew-call-for-permanent-un-security-council-seats",
    "summary": "At the 81st UN General Assembly, African countries reiterated demands for at least two permanent and five non-permanent Security Council seats, but reform remains stalled.",
    "content": "During the 81st session of the United Nations General Assembly, African states have once again pressed for reform of the UN Security Council, calling for Africa to be allocated no fewer than two permanent seats and five non-permanent seats. The demand reflects long-standing frustrations that the current Council structure does not reflect today's political and geographical realities.\n\nThe push for reform is not new. For years, African nations have argued that the Security Council, established in 1945, is outdated and unrepresentative, particularly given that Africa is the largest regional group in the UN with 54 member states. The Ezulwini Consensus, adopted by the African Union in 2005, formally articulated this position, seeking two permanent seats with veto rights and five non-permanent seats. Despite repeated advocacy, progress has been slow due to resistance from existing permanent members and competing claims among African states over who would occupy the seats.\n\nUN Secretary-General António Guterres has repeatedly supported calls for reform, stating that the Council must adapt to the changing global landscape. However, any amendment to the UN Charter requires approval by two-thirds of the General Assembly and ratification by two-thirds of member states, including all five permanent members—a high bar that has historically blocked change.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, this issue is particularly significant. Somalia has long been a subject of Security Council deliberations, from sanctions regimes to peacekeeping missions. A more representative Council could lead to greater attention to African conflicts and more legitimate decisions affecting the continent. Many in the diaspora advocate for reform as a step toward a more equitable international system that addresses African concerns more effectively.\n\nAs the debate continues, African leaders remain steadfast in their demand, but the path to reform is fraught with obstacles. The coming years will test whether the international community can overcome longstanding divisions and make the Security Council fit for the 21st century.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/26/afrika-oo-dalbanaysa-kuraas-joogto-ah-golaha-ammaanka-ah-balse-dib-u-habayntu-weli-caqabad-ku-tahay/",
